\details{
\strong{Reading numbers with \code{destring()}}

Text must contain a complete number. Signs, exponents, dollar/euro/pound/yen
symbols, thousands separators (\code{"1,234"}), trailing percent signs (\code{"50\%"}
gives 50) and surrounding spaces, including non-breaking spaces, are accepted.
Use \code{ignore} to remove other characters first: \code{ignore = "kg"} lets
\code{"10 kg"} become 10.

Invalid text becomes \code{NA} with a warning, like Stata's \code{force} option.
\code{\link[readr:problems]{readr::problems()}} gives its positions and original values. Empty strings,
\code{"NA"} and \code{"."} become missing without a warning; \code{"NaN"} becomes \code{NaN}.
Date strings are invalid; parse them with \code{\link[=as.Date]{as.Date()}} or \code{lubridate::ymd()}.

Factors give their numeric level values only when every level, including
unused levels, parses as a number. Otherwise they give their integer codes.
Labelled vectors use their stored values, parsing text as above. SPSS
user-missing codes remain values, and tagged missing values retain their tags.
Lists, raw vectors and complex vectors give \code{NA} for each element.

Dates become days since 1970-01-01; date-times become seconds since the Unix
epoch. Durations and measured values keep their numbers in the existing
units. Large \code{integer64} values can lose precision when converted to double,
with a warning from the underlying conversion.

\strong{Creating labels with \code{encode()}}

Character categories receive codes 1, 2, 3, ... in UTF-8 radix order, which
is reproducible but may differ from Stata's locale-based ordering.
Numeric-looking text is still a category: \code{"10"}, \code{"20"}, \code{"3"} receive
codes 1, 2, 3. \code{NA} and \code{""} become missing; spaces are retained.

Factors keep their integer codes and use all levels as labels, including
unused, empty-string and explicit \code{NA} levels. For example,
\code{factor(c(10, 20))} keeps codes 1 and 2. Ordered factors lose their ordering
metadata.

Encode character columns before grouping so every group uses the same
codes. Separate calls can assign different codes; factors with shared
levels keep consistent codes in grouped \code{dplyr::mutate()}. You cannot supply
an existing label dictionary. Re-encoding decoded text creates a new
dictionary, so \code{encode(decode(x))} may change the original codes.

\strong{Reading labels with \code{decode()}}

\code{decode()} uses value labels when available and factor levels for factors.
Values without a label remain as text, whereas Stata turns them into empty
strings. SPSS user-missing codes remain values or labels.

Inputs without value labels use ordinary \code{\link[=as.character]{as.character()}} conversion,
including for tagged missing values. Thus 100000 may become \code{"1e+05"};
use \code{tostring()} to write numbers in full. Unlike Stata's \code{decode}, this
function also accepts factors, labelled character vectors and unlabelled
inputs.

\strong{Writing text with \code{tostring()}}

\code{tostring()} ignores value labels and converts factor codes to text.
Numbers are written in full (\code{100000} becomes \code{"100000"}), with the
significant digits of \code{\link[=as.character]{as.character()}} and a decimal point regardless of
\code{options(OutDec)}. Distinct large numbers can produce the same text; keep
identifiers as character or \code{integer64} vectors when exact digits matter.

For non-character input, values flagged by \code{is.na()} become \code{NA}, including
\code{NaN}, tagged missing values and SPSS user-missing codes. Stata instead
writes missing values as \code{"."} or \code{".a"} through \code{".z"}. Character input is
unchanged. AsIs wrappers are ignored for other inputs; durations and
measured values use their existing units. Dates use \code{\link[=as.character]{as.character()}},
for example \code{"2024-01-31"}.

The aliases \code{as_numeric()} and \code{as_character()} also exist in sjlabelled,
with different behavior: its numeric conversion codes non-numeric text as
factors, and its character conversion makes unlabelled values \code{NA}. Use
\code{tidystata::as_numeric()} or the names \code{destring()} and \code{tostring()} to
avoid depending on which package was attached last.
